                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "equitek":</h3><p><b>Equitek</b> is CAD&#8217;s name for a unique microphone amplifier circuit design. </p><p>The original Equitek circuit, found in the 1990s-era E-100, E-200, and E-300 microphones as well as the revised e100<sup>2</sup> and e300<sup>2</sup>, employed opamps rather than transformers or transistors in the output stage. This was done to avoid the &#8220;inherently nonlinear&#8221; behavior of output circuits with twin output transistors, which are difficult to match precisely.</p><p>The drawback to op-amps is their high current draw. Standard 48V phantom power is insufficient to power these output ICs (such as TI&#8217;s OPA2107) during transients. Therefore, CAD reengineered the power supply to feed the high-current op-amps in the Equitek output circuit: P48 was used to trickle-charge two built-in rechargeable (NiCd or NiMH) 9V batteries, which in turn powered the amplifier circuit.</p><p>The next-generation Equitek circuit, such as found in the e60, retained the op-amps but did not require internal batteries. (CAD&#8217;s M179, although not named Equitek, employs a similar phantom-powered, no-battery op-amp output circuit &#8212; and requires true P48 power for best operation.)</p><p>With the release of the E100S in 2009, the Equitek name has evolved again, and in a sense come full-circle. The Equitek E100S has a full-differential design with 4 FETs on the input and two bipolar transistors (but no op-amps) on the output. The E100S does not have rechargeable batteries in the circuit, and is able to deliver full performance at low current draw.</p><p>So, the latest Equitek design reverts to discrete output transistors &#8212; and whatever nonlinearities they bring &#8212; in exchange for lower self-noise (3.7dBA!) and low current draw.</p>
